@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' Destination diagram — prose from internal/choiceui/choiceui.go and cmd/proveo/main.go's
' row-gating helpers (applicableRows, comingSoon, gateAddons, gateEvidence) lands here (batches 3 and 6).
' What the answers MEAN and how they are cached: _spec/internal/agentsettings/choice-cache.puml.
title Choice prompt render — radio axes above the divider, checkboxes below

node "internal/choiceui" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "Form\nBanner · Title · Header · Rows" as FORM <<app>>
  component "Row\nradio: Options + Selected\ncheckbox: Multi + On/Off" as ROW <<app>>
  component "draw(screen, cursor)\nlegend · divider · markers" as DRAW <<app>>
  component "Run()\ntcell screen, one key per action" as RUN <<app>>
  component "OnChange(*Form)\nre-gated after every keystroke" as CHANGE <<app>>
  component "Selection · Selections\nread back by label" as READ <<app>>
}

node "cmd/proveo — what may be offered" as GATES <<host>> {
  component "applicableRows\ndrop an axis with nothing to decide" as APPL <<app>>
  component "comingSoon\ngrey out + move the selection off" as SOON <<app>>
  component "gateAddons · gateEvidence\ncross-row exclusivity" as GATE <<app>>
}

component "Operator terminal\nrequires a TTY" as TTY <<host>>
component "EnvHeader\ndetected secrets · settings" as HDR <<app>>
component "A locked or absent row read as an answer" as MISREAD <<error>>

GATES ARROW_MAIN FORM : rows built, then filtered
HDR ARROW_MAIN FORM
FORM ARROW_MAIN ROW
FORM ARROW_MAIN DRAW
RUN ARROW_MAIN DRAW : redraw per event
RUN ARROW_MAIN CHANGE
CHANGE ARROW_MAIN GATE
APPL ARROW_MAIN FORM
SOON ARROW_MAIN ROW
DRAW ARROW_MAIN TTY
TTY ARROW_MAIN RUN : Enter confirms · Esc/q cancels
RUN ARROW_MAIN READ
READ ARROW_ERROR MISREAD : fall back to the resolved value instead

note bottom of DRAW
  **The divider separates two kinds of question.** Above it the radio axes,
  ordered riskier → safer by the legend the draw pass prints; below it the
  checkbox toggles, which that ordering does not describe.

  **""agent evidence"" is a toggle, not a tier.** It sits with the add-ons rather
  than under the legend because narrating more of the run costs tokens and
  screen, not safety — nothing about it is riskier or safer. ""evidenceRow"" draws
  the two levels as checkboxes with ""verbose"" ticked, and the pair is kept
  exclusive by ""gateEvidence"".
end note

note right of GATE
  **One gate per toggle row, run unconditionally.** ""gateAddons"" no-ops when its
  row was filtered out, so both gates can fire on every keystroke instead of the
  caller tracking which rows made it onto the form.

  ""gateEvidence"" keeps the two evidence boxes exclusive: a level is one answer,
  so ticking one clears the other. Clearing BOTH is allowed and reads as
  ""default"" — ""evidenceFrom"" asks only whether verbose was chosen.
end note

note bottom of SOON
  **Greyed, never hidden.** ""comingSoon"" marks the option off and moves the
  selection away from it, but the row still shows it with the reason attached:
  removing it would misrepresent the tier as nonexistent rather than unfinished.

  ""--egress-mode"" still accepts the value, so development can drive an
  unfinished tier while an operator cannot pick it by accident.

  ""review"" is greyed by its platform rather than its state, and the row names
  which constraint failed — 'linux only' or 'needs a local docker daemon'. The
  transport reason is in ""_spec/internal/reviewgate/pty-review-proxy\.puml"".
end note

note bottom of MISREAD
  **A filtered-out axis has no row to read, and empty is not an answer.** The
  gate falls back to the value already resolved from the manifest. Without that
  fallback, hiding cursor's single-option rows made the gate see empty strings
  and wrongly disable ""dind"" — on the one harness whose fixed tier can host it.
end note

note left of TTY
  **The add-ons picker runs BEFORE the missing-env wizard, and the order is
  load-bearing.** The wizard may attach a ""bufio\.Reader"" to stdin, and a
  buffered reader starves an interactive picker of the keystrokes it is waiting
  for. So the multiselect is answered first, while stdin is still raw.
end note

note right of TTY
  **""isStdinTTY"" is the one gate in front of every interactive prompt** — the
  monorepo scope picker and the missing-env wizard both ask it — so no prompt
  can decide on its own that a run is interactive.

  It is a real ioctl check (""term\.IsTerminal"" on an ""*os\.File"" descriptor),
  **not** a char-device stat. ""/dev/null"" is a character device too, so a stat
  would read a stdin redirected to null as an operator waiting to answer and
  block a run that can never reply. A reader that is not an ""*os\.File"" at all
  — a test's ""strings\.Reader"" — is likewise not a terminal, which is what
  lets the scope picker fall back to its numbered form under test.
end note

note right of HDR
  **Models are shown, not chosen.** The grid holds closed choices and a model id
  is an open value, so the roles are printed in the header — beside the git repo,
  the loaded ""\.env"" and the workspace layout — and never become a row.

  Each role carries the provider it resolved to. That is what makes a keyless
  role visible BEFORE launch rather than after a failed call.
end note
note bottom of ROW
  **The legend describes only the radio rows, which is why it is drawn
  once, above them.** Both policy axes are ordered riskier → safer, so a
  single label explains every one of them and the operator does not have to
  infer direction from option names. A row that is a checkbox set carries no
  such order, so the centred divider closes the axis rather than letting the
  legend appear to govern the whole prompt — and only the row the divider is
  named after drops its own label, or a second checkbox section would render
  as an anonymous pair of boxes.

  **The consent modal takes the whole screen rather than composing with
  this form.** It answers one connection at a time, mid-run, over a
  suspended agent TUI — a different question from the one-shot posture
  prompt, and sharing layout would couple two lifecycles that have nothing
  else in common.
end note


@enduml
